{"uid":"3c0c74953fbd3ee0","name":"test_no_mid_plan_review_episode_starts_at_an_already_expired_boundary[iterations]","fullName":"tests.test_mid_plan_review_episode#test_no_mid_plan_review_episode_starts_at_an_already_expired_boundary","historyId":"92f5b1d6c60222cbc225d36838631235","time":{"start":1792160927034,"stop":1792160927078,"duration":44},"description":"Task cure-01-03 (v0.24 requirement A.1/K.2, DoD 1-3, #169): the\n    episode is a bare async call from inside `_step_worker`'s own `while\n    self.budget_left():` loop BODY, so the loop's own boundary check (only\n    re-consulted at the top of the next turn) does not stand between a\n    worker iteration that just spent the last of the budget and this call.\n    Requirement A.1 forbids starting a new iteration there: an\n    already-expired boundary must dispatch NO mid-plan review iteration --\n    and the pass must still land in the shared `exhausted` freeze path\n    (`_budget_exhausted_tail` -> `_finish_tail_unverified` ->\n    `_finish_exhausted`, task 010/011's own C.1 path) with a null verdict.\n\n    Driven through the REAL `_step_worker` call site, with the worker\n    iteration itself spending the last of the budget -- the exact ordering\n    the guard exists for -- over both halves of `budget_left()`: an\n    expired job deadline and an exhausted iteration budget. Everything\n    else about the episode is left DUE (flag on, fraction reached,\n    marker unspent, a blocking finding waiting on disk), so the absence of\n    a dispatch can only be the boundary: `test_flag_on_runs_one_scoped_\n    episode_before_verify_and_opens_a_cure_task` above dispatches one from\n    this very fixture. The in-flight counterpart -- a review already\n    dispatched while budget WAS available finishes and its verdict is\n    applied -- is pinned in `tests/test_deadline.py`'s Sections I/J, with\n    the ordinary run-ending review's own no-dispatch twin in that file's\n    Section Jb.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>Task cure-01-03 (v0.24 requirement A.1/K.2, DoD 1-3, #169): the<br />\nepisode is a bare async call from inside <code>_step_worker</code>'s own <code>while self.budget_left():</code> loop BODY, so the loop's own boundary check (only<br />\nre-consulted at the top of the next turn) does not stand between a<br />\nworker iteration that just spent the last of the budget and this call.<br />\nRequirement A.1 forbids starting a new iteration there: an<br />\nalready-expired boundary must dispatch NO mid-plan review iteration --<br />\nand the pass must still land in the shared <code>exhausted</code> freeze path<br />\n(<code>_budget_exhausted_tail</code> -&gt; <code>_finish_tail_unverified</code> -&gt;<br />\n<code>_finish_exhausted</code>, task 010/011's own C.1 path) with a null verdict.</p>\n<pre><code>Driven through the REAL `_step_worker` call site, with the worker\niteration itself spending the last of the budget -- the exact ordering\nthe guard exists for -- over both halves of `budget_left()`: an\nexpired job deadline and an exhausted iteration budget. Everything\nelse about the episode is left DUE (flag on, fraction reached,\nmarker unspent, a blocking finding waiting on disk), so the absence of\na dispatch can only be the boundary: `test_flag_on_runs_one_scoped_\nepisode_before_verify_and_opens_a_cure_task` above dispatches one from\nthis very fixture. The in-flight counterpart -- a review already\ndispatched while budget WAS available finishes and its verdict is\napplied -- is pinned in `tests/test_deadline.py`'s Sections I/J, with\nthe ordinary run-ending review's own no-dispatch twin in that file's\nSection Jb.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1792160405870,"stop":1792160405871,"duration":1},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCoun